# Job: spec deep review R6-fw-spot-actor (read-only diagnosis, report only)

You are a codex sub-agent under a Claude supervisor. Model gpt-6-astra, reasoning xhigh. Cap 3 h.
Read `AGENTS.md` (root) §2/§3/§5 and `doc/plan/c016-worklog/spec-review/README.ko.md` first — the README
fixes the review criteria, the findings template and the job split; follow it exactly.

## Hard rules
- READ-ONLY. Do not modify, create, move or delete any file except your one report file
  `doc/plan/c016-worklog/spec-review/R6-fw-spot-actor-summary.md`. Never touch `core/**`, `bindings/**`,
  `framework/**`, any `doc/spec/**`, `framework/doc/**`, `doc/site/**`. Do not run builds, tests,
  benchmarks or git commands that change state (`git diff`/`git log`/`git grep` are fine). Do not commit.
- Evidence, not opinion: every finding cites spec `file:line` for *every* place the rule is stated and
  implementation `file:line` per language you examined. If you could not verify a language, say so.
- One finding = one cause. Do not propose mitigations (timeouts, retries, extra pollers, catch-alls).
  Every proposal is "N rules → M rules" with the draft sentence of the merged rule.
- Mark `행동 변경: 있음` whenever an application could observe a difference; the supervisor schedules
  those for 0.18.0 and applies only `없음` items now — be precise.
- Do not rewrite spec text at length; a proposal is one merged sentence plus where it lives.

## Scope (read all of it)
`framework/doc/framework/common/spec/server/03-spot-actor/*.ko.md` (spot model, spot messaging, mesh node, actor model, spot/actor membership, address messaging, stage wrapper, routing, object lifecycle, spot timer) (≈5,700 lines)

Implementation evidence: `framework/languages/{cpp,dotnet,java,node,kotlin}/**` mesh node / actor / spot runtimes (e.g. dotnet `ZLinkManagedMeshNode.cs`, java `ZLinkJavaRawMeshNode.java`, cpp `raw_mesh_node_owner.cpp`, node mesh runtime)

## What to look for in this scope
- Durable operation lifecycle (actor-model §9 replay rules, §8.1 D-093 rules, 08-routing §2.6 owner-terminated → `Unavailable`, membership §2 reservation/terminal record): the same operation's completion is described in at least three documents. Map every sentence that decides *when a durable operation ends* and propose the single owner (`scattered-control`), keeping rule count minimal.
- Mesh node §7.1 (connection intent rules, D-100/D-098-8), §8 drain, relocation §14 seal (R7) and D-097: "does this node start/accept admission?" — count the deciders.
- Actor-model §9 currently holds replay *rules* (prose) inside the verification section — a §9.3 violation; propose where each moves (form), without changing behavior.
- Object lifecycle / generation (`09-object-lifecycle`, routing §2.6): ObjectGeneration, lifecycle generation, descriptor revision, incarnation — four version-like values. Are all four observable and needed by a caller? Which comparisons does the runtime actually perform (cite code per language)? (`complexity`)
- Spot timer / stage wrapper: check they only reference the execution model rather than redefining turn/gate rules.

## Quality bar
`doc/principal/documentation/spec-writing-guide.ko.md` §1 (reader questions), §2.4/§4.4 (bold rule +
reason; contract vs implementation narrative; `**언어별 재량**` only with the parity reason), §4.3 (one
layer per rule), §9.3 (verification section = interface observations only). Campaign rules:
`doc/plan/c016-worklog/decisions.ko.md` D-090…D-101 are the freshest decisions; the supervisor's
principles are "one owner per rule", "simple = fewer rules", "no mitigations".

## Deliverable
`doc/plan/c016-worklog/spec-review/R6-fw-spot-actor-summary.md`, in Korean (identifiers/quotes verbatim), with:
1. 요약 표 (sorted per README priority; columns 번호·제목·분류·행동 변경·규칙 수·성능 영향·확신).
2. Findings `### F-R6-<k>` in the fixed template, max 20; extra candidates as one-liners.
3. `## 읽은 범위` — files and line counts actually read; anything skipped and why.
4. `## BLOCKERS` — questions only the supervisor can answer (spec gaps you must not decide).
Finish by writing the report; do not print it to stdout.
